      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pawnee%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The area was opened to non-Indian settlers on September 16, 1893, during the Cherokee Outlet Opening.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pawnee%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Railroads enabled Pawnee to develop as an agricultural trade center during the early twentieth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pawnee%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The federal government built Pawnee Lake two miles north of town in 1932, creating over five miles of shoreline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pawnee%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pawnee is the birthplace of Chester Gould, the cartoonist who created the famous comic strip Dick Tracy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
